📘 Project Standard DocumentationTable of Contents1. Purpose of Application2. Tools & Technologies3. Architecture4. Development Lifecycle5. Branching Strategy6. Getting Started1. Purpose of the ApplicationThe [Project Name] is a modular web application built on the Django framework. It serves as a centralized platform to [Insert Primary Business Goal, e.g., automate ServiceNow ticket workflows and provide a unified dashboard].Core Objectives:Modular Growth: Structure allows for rapid addition of new Django apps without side effects.Third-Party Extensibility: Dedicated integrations/ layer for external API management (e.g., ServiceNow).Standardization: Uniform code quality and deployment patterns across the development team.2. Tools & TechnologiesWe utilize a modern Python stack (2026 Standards) to ensure security, type safety, and performance.CategoryToolDescriptionFrameworkDjango 5.x+Enterprise-grade Python web framework.Linter/FormatterRuffRust-based engine for linting and code formatting.SecurityBanditStatic analysis tool to find common security issues.Type SafetyMypyStatic type checker to prevent runtime logic errors.DatabasePostgreSQLRelational database for production data.API ClientRequests/HttpxFor snow.py and other integration modules.3. ArchitectureThe project structure is designed to separate Business Logic from System Integrations.my_project/: Project configuration and settings.app1/ ... appN/: Individual business units. Logic should stay within these boundaries.integrations/: This is a Non-Django App folder. It houses pure Python classes for external services.Rule: Never put Django Models in the integrations/ folder. Keep it stateless.4. Development LifecycleAll developers must follow this workflow to ensure code quality and system stability.Step 1: Local SetupCreate a branch from develop.Install dependencies: pip install -r requirements.txt.Set up your local .env file (Do not commit this).Step 2: Quality Assurance (The "Pre-Flight" Check)Before pushing any code, you must run the following suite:Bash# 1. Format and Lint
ruff check . --fix

# 2. Check Types
mypy .

# 3. Security Scan
bandit -r .

# 4. Run Tests
python manage.py test
Step 3: Pull Request (PR) ProcessPush your branch to Bitbucket.Open a PR targeting the develop branch.Link the relevant Jira/Trello ticket in the description.Peer Review: At least one approval is required before merging.5. Branching StrategyWe use a Feature-Branch workflow to manage the codebase:main: Production-ready code. Locked for direct commits.develop: The "Bleeding Edge." All new features are integrated here.feature/*: Individual features or task-based development.hotfix/*: Emergency patches created from main and merged back to both main and develop.6. Getting StartedTo get the project running locally:Bashgit clone <bitbucket-repo-url>
python -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
python manage.py migrate
python manage.py runserver